1. Sealed Classes

How it was before? Java inheritance was completely open. Any class could extend another unless explicitly prevented. Or using final that means nobody can extend it.
This made it difficult to enforce domain rules.

What changed in Java 17?
Java 17 introduced sealed classes, allowing a class or interface to explicitly restrict which classes can extend or implement it.
                  public sealed class Shape
                          permits Circle, Rectangle {
                  }        
With this declaration:
- Only Circle and Rectangle can extend Shape
- Any other attempt to extend Shape results in a compile-time error

Rules to Follow When Using Sealed Classes?
Rule 1: A sealed class must declare its permitted subclasses
                public sealed class Shape
                        permits Circle, Rectangle {
                }        
The permits list defines all allowed direct subclasses. No class outside this list can extend Shape

Rule 2: Every permitted subclass must declare its inheritance behavior
Each permitted subclass must be marked as exactly one of:
✔ final -Prevents further inheritance       
✔ sealed-Continues controlled inheritance at the next level       
✔ non-sealed-Reopens inheritance for that branch
              public non-sealed class Triangle extends Shape {
              }        
If a permitted subclass does not declare one of these, the code will not compile.

Rule 3: Permitted subclasses must be known at compile time
Permitted subclasses must be:
  In the same package, or
  In the same module
This ensures the compiler can fully verify the hierarchy.

Rule 4: Sealed classes can also be interfaces. The same rules apply as sealed classes.

For example, in a payment system, I might have a sealed PaymentResult with Success, Failure, and Pending implementations. 
Success can contain a transaction ID, Failure can contain an error message, and Pending can contain pending information. 
This is more expressive than an enum when different states require different data.

